 but I don't have them. W3 and W5 could be part of a chow if I have W4. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can go for a mixed straight. For example, having chows in different suits. But I'm not sure. \n\nAnother option is to look for All Chows, which is 2 points. If I can form four chows and a pair. Let's see. If I have B7-8-9 as a chow, then maybe T3-4-5 (but I don't have T4 or T5). W3-4-5 (I don't have W4). So maybe not. \n\nWait, the previous player has a meld of B2, B3, B4. So if I have B5, maybe I can form a chow with B3, B4, B5 if I had B3 and B4. But I don't. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a pung of W3s. If I can get another W3. The unshown tiles have 3 W3s left. So if I hold onto my two W3s, maybe drawing another. \n\nBut I need to decide which tile to discard. The valid actions are Play T6, F2, B7, B9, B5, W5, B8, T7, W3, T3, F4, W1. \n\nIf I discard T6, maybe the next player can use it for a chow. They have T5, T6, T7 already. But they already have a chow there. Maybe not. \n\nIf I discard B7, then I lose a chance to form the B7-8-9 chow. Similarly for B8 or B9. But if I discard one of them, maybe I can form a pair later. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile that's not part of any important meld, like F2 or F4. Since I have F2 and F4 as single tiles. Discarding one of them might not affect my hand much. \n\nBut considering the unshown tiles, F2 and F4 have 4 each left. So discarding them might not be risky. \n\nLooking at the hand, the B7, B8, B9 are all present. If I discard one, say B7, then I can't form that chow. But if I keep them all, maybe I can form that chow. Since forming a chow would help towards All Chows or other patterns. \n\nSo maybe the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of a potential chow. For example, F2 or F4. Since I have only one each, and they are honor tiles. Discarding F2 would leave me with F4. \n\nAlternatively, maybe discard T6. Since I have two T7s, maybe building towards a pair of T7s. If I discard T6, then I have T