hey are already used. Therefore, my actual hand tiles are separate. So I don't have T2 and T3 in my hand. So I can't form a T1-T2-T3 chow. So the only way to form a fourth chow is to have three consecutive tiles in a suit. Given my current hand, the only possibility is if I can get T6 to form T6-T7-T8. So if I discard a tile, I can keep T7 and T8 and wait for T6. Alternatively, if I discard B3, I can keep T1, T7, T8 and wait for T6.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T7, I have T1, T8, B3. Then I need to form a chow with T1-T2-T3 or B3-B4-B5, but I don't have those. So maybe keeping T7 and T8 is better for forming a chow with T6.\n\nSo the best strategy is to discard a tile that doesn't block my own potential chow. If I discard B3, I keep T1, T7, T8. If I can get T6, I can form T6-T7-T8. If I discard T1, I keep T7, T8, B3. Still need T6. If I discard T8, I keep T1, T7, B3. Need T6. If I discard T7, I keep T1, T8, B3. Need T6. So all options require T6. But which discard is safer for others?\n\nIf I discard T7, the next player might have T6 and T8 to chi. The next player's melds include T5-T6-T7, so they might have T6. If they have T6 and T8, they can chi with T7. The player across has a meld of T3-T4-T5, so they might have T5 but not T6. The previous player's melds don't include Dots in the higher numbers. So the next player is the most likely to chi if I discard T7.\n\nIf I discard B3, the previous player has a meld of B2-B3-B4, so they might have already used those tiles. If they have B2 and B4, they could chi with B3. But since they have a meld of B2-B3-B4, they might have already used all three, so they can't chi again. So discarding B3 might be safer.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to discard B3, as it's less likely to be needed for others to chi, and it keeps my Dots suit tiles (T1, T7, T8) in case I can form a chow with T6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding B3 is optimal as it minimizes the risk of others completing a chi while preserving potential for a Dots chow.]\nReason: [B3 is part of a lower Bamboo chow (B2-B3-B4) already exposed in the previous player's meld, reducing its utility for others. Keeping T7 and T8 in Dots allows forming T6-T7-T8 if T6 is drawn, while discarding B3 avoids giving others a chance to chi with B2/B4.]\nAnswer: Play B3"}
