plete their B7-B8-B9 sequence. But they already have a B7 in their meld. So maybe they don't need it. Alternatively, if they have a B7 in their hand, they might need B8 or B9. But I'm not sure. Similarly, playing T7 or T8 might help the player across, who has a T3-T4-T5 meld and might need T6 or T7. Playing T7 would give them T7, which could complete T6-T7-T8 if they have T6 and T8. But I don't know their hand.\n\nAlternatively, playing W9. The unshown W9 has 1 left. If I play it, others might need it for a terminal. But since I'm the North Wind and the prevailing wind is South, maybe W9 is part of a terminal. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, playing T2. The unshown T2 has 1 left. If I play it, others might need it for a sequence. But the melds of others don't seem to involve T2 much.\n\nIn this situation, the safest play is to discard a tile that is least likely to help others. Since all are rare, but B7 is part of a sequence that the next player has, maybe avoiding that. So perhaps playing W9, as it's a terminal and less likely to be part of a sequence others need. Alternatively, playing T2, which is part of the T2-T3-T4 sequence already in my melds. But since T3 and T4 are already in a meld, maybe T2 is less needed.\n\nBut the problem is that all options are risky. Given that, I need to choose the least risky. Maybe playing B7 is the worst because the next player has a B7 in their meld. Playing T7 or T8 might help the player across. Playing T2 might help the previous player. W9 is a terminal, maybe less useful. So the best choice is to play W9.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has three Chis and five tiles. To form a winning hand, a fourth meld or pair is needed. Playing W9 minimizes risk as it's a terminal tile less likely to complete others' sequences.\nReason: W9 is a terminal tile with only one remaining in the wall, reducing the chance of aiding opponents. Other tiles (T2, T7, T8, B7) are part of sequences in existing melds or opponents' hands, increasing risk.\nAnswer: Play W9"}
